# Photorealistic 3D Visualizer Implementation Plan

You are completely right. The Plotly heat-map graph is too abstract. To make it truly understandable, we need to map the **actual RGB photograph** onto the 3D depth geometry, so it literally looks like a piece of the road popping out of the screen.

Plotly is not well-suited for rendering photorealistic textures, so we will upgrade the 3D engine to **Three.js** (via React Three Fiber).

## 1. Backend Data Pipeline (`api.py`)
Instead of sending a massive array of numbers, we will utilize native GPU displacement mapping.
- **[MODIFY] `api.py`**: We will generate a clean, grayscale version of the depth map (without the Inferno colors) and encode it as a base64 image (`img_depth_gray`).
- This is incredibly lightweight compared to sending JSON arrays and is the exact format 3D engines expect.

## 2. Frontend Dependencies
- **[RUN]**: `npm install three @react-three/fiber @react-three/drei` in the `web-ui` directory.
- We will remove the clunky Plotly dependencies.

## 3. Frontend Component (`web-ui/src/components/detection/`)

#### [NEW] `Textured3DVisualizer.jsx`
- We will create a Three.js Canvas.
- We will construct a high-resolution 3D plane (`PlaneGeometry`).
- We will apply the `original` RGB image as the primary texture (`map`).
- We will apply the new `depth_gray` image as the `displacementMap`.
- This tells the GPU to physically push the pixels of the road image up and down based on the depth values, creating a perfect, photorealistic 3D replica of the pothole.
- We will add `OrbitControls` so you can effortlessly pan, zoom, and rotate the physical chunk of road.

#### [MODIFY] `InteractiveViewport.jsx`
- Replace the Plotly component with the new `Textured3DVisualizer`.

